<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden. <b>Theme:</b> The Pink Flowers!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or body language to describe plants and trees to classmates (e.g., miming being a tree by stretching the arms upward or pretending to hold a bouquet of flowers). Can clarify responses about nature by pointing to garden objects or drawing pictures (e.g., drawing a simple flower or a tree on the board). Can use simple gestures or drawings to explain feelings about the garden (e.g., pointing to a tall tree in response to the question, "What do you like most in the garden?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in "Garden Mime Challenge." The teacher shows flashcards of garden items (e.g., `flower`, `tree`, `leaf`, `seed`) and mimes the action or characteristic. Students call out the word or mime along. For example, for "tall tree," the teacher stretches arms up high; for "small seed," the teacher curls into a ball. This activates prior vocabulary and introduces gesture-based communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe a garden object using a word and a gesture. The teacher holds up a picture of a `pink flower`. "Look! This is a `flower` (points to the picture). It is `pink` (points to something pink in the classroom or mimes drawing a circle)." Then, the teacher holds up a picture of a `tall tree`. "This is a `tree` (points). It is `tall` (stretches arms up high, emphasizing /t/ in `tree`)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my words to say 'flower'?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my hands to show 'tall'?" (Students respond: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me 'big'?" (Students mime 'big'.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher shows simple, pre-drawn examples of "Garden Nature Journal" entries from previous lessons (simplified versions with basic drawings and labels like "This is a `tree`." or "The `flower` is `pink`."). The teacher points to each element and uses gestures to reinforce meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards or projected images of simple drawings from mock-up "Garden Nature Journal" entries showing `flower`, `tree`, `plant`, `leaf`, each with a simple descriptive sentence (`Noun + verb + adjective`, `Present simple`) and a visual cue. E.g., a drawing of a green leaf with "This is a `leaf`. It is `green`." The /s/ sound in `sun` is highlighted when discussing light in the garden.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about nature: Describe plants and trees with singular phrases (e.g., "The `tree` is `tall`." "The `flower` is `pink`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a drawing of a flower: "Is this a `flower` or a `tree`?" (Students point or say "flower").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a drawing of a `pink flower`: "`What color` is the `flower`?" (Students say "Pink!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a `tall tree` drawing: "Is this `tall` or `short`?" (Students mime `tall` or `short`).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The `flowers` are `pink`."), WH-questions (e.g., "`What color` is the `flower`?"), Present simple (e.g., "This is a `tall tree`."). Using gestures and simple drawings for clarification and description (Mediation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Garden Mime and Match:</b> The teacher says a garden word (e.g., "`grow`," "`tall` `tree`" emphasizing /g/ in `garden` and /t/ in `tree`) and mimes it. Students find the corresponding picture flashcard and mime back. Then, the teacher shows a picture (e.g., `seed`) and students say the word and mime planting a `seed`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Draw and Describe:</b> Students receive small whiteboards or paper. The teacher says a word, e.g., "`pink flower`" or "`green leaf`." Students draw the item quickly. Then, in pairs, one student shows their drawing and says, "This is `my flower`," and uses a gesture (e.g., `pink` flower, `small` flower) to describe it to their partner. The partner identifies it or asks "`What color` is `your flower`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Mystery Garden Object:</b> Students work in pairs. Student A chooses a garden object from a set of flashcards (e.g., `sun`, `bush`, `vine`). They describe it to Student B using only one word and one gesture or pointing (e.g., for `sun`: points up and says "`sun`" or for `vine`: wiggles hand like a `vine` `grow`ing). Student B guesses. They then switch roles. This activity practices `respect for nature` through careful communication about plants.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Garden Nature Journal Explanation." Students will share and explain one entry from their "Garden Nature Journal" (or a simplified pre-prepared drawing if journals are not complete) to a small group of classmates (3-4 students). The focus is on using mediation strategies (gestures, pointing, simple drawings) to clarify their descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students bring their "Garden Nature Journal" entries (or a chosen drawing/photo from the school garden exploration). They will act as "garden guides" explaining their observations to their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups. Each student takes turns being the "presenter" of their journal entry.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The presenter shows their drawing (e.g., a `tall tree`, emphasizing the /t/ sound). They use simple sentences: "This is a `tree`." "It is `tall`." "It is `green`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">To `mediate` their message, they must use a gesture or body language (e.g., stretch arms up for `tall`, point to the `tree` in the drawing) or draw a quick sketch on a mini-whiteboard if a classmate doesn't understand. They might also point to the relevant part of their drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Classmates can ask simple `WH-questions` like "`What color` is it?" or point to clarify. The presenter uses mediation skills to answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Student: "This is a `flower` (points to drawing). It is `pink` (mimes drawing a `pink` flower in the air)." Classmate: "`Small` `flower`?" Student: "Yes! It is `small` (shows `small` with hands)." This fosters `respect for nature` by sharing observations.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher circulates among groups during the "My Garden Nature Journal Explanation" task, noting student use of mediation strategies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student explains one specific entry from their "Garden Nature Journal" to the teacher or a peer, using words and at least one gesture or drawing to clarify their description. (e.g., "This is `my plant` (points). It can `grow` (mimes `grow`ing)." `Respect for nature` is assessed by observing their care in explaining their findings.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /g/ in `garden`, /t/ in `tree`, /s/ in `sun`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., `flower`, `tree`, `pink`, `tall`, `grow`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of description using simple sentences (`Noun + verb + adjective`, `Present simple`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successful use of gestures, body language, or pointing to clarify meaning to classmates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to a simple `WH-question` about their journal entry using mediation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my hands to help my friends understand `my flower`?" (Circle Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I tell `my friend` one thing about the `garden`?" (Circle Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like most about showing `your garden` drawing with `your hands`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to show with `your hands`? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? Students were engaged when using gestures and drawings. The "Garden Nature Journal Explanation" allowed for authentic use of mediation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with using gestures or simple sentences? More one-on-one modeling, visual prompts for gestures, or sentence frames like "This is a _____. It is _____." would be helpful. Focus on individual vocabulary for those struggling with pronunciation (e.g., `flower` vs. `flour`).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "It's Our Garden: The Pink Flowers!" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and scenario, building on the basic communicative skills acquired.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>